  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>According to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>MO</mi></math> theory, number of species/ions from the following having identical bond order is____</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>CN</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>NO</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "3",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The bond order of a diatomic species can be calculated using molecular orbital theory as follows,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Bond</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>order</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>No</mi><mo>.</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>BMO</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>electrons</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>No</mi><mo>.</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>ABMO</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>electrons</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>BMO = bonding molecular orbital</p><p>ABMO = Anti bonding molecular orbital.</p><p>Based on the total number electrons in bond orders are given in the following table.</p><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"1\" cellspacing=\"1\" style=\"width: 200px;\">\t<tbody>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math></td>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\">Bond order</td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>13</mn></math></td>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>14</mn></math></td>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn></math></td>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t\t<tr>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math></td>\t\t\t<td style=\"text-align: center;\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math></td>\t\t</tr>\t</tbody></table><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>CN</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>NO</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msubsup></math>&nbsp;are having 14 electrons each, hence the bond order is 3.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}